#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# media-licence - Ágora - unit 003, wave 9, task T-904.
#
# Every non-YAML, non-JSON file shipped under content/ - a photograph, a PDF, a
# CSV distribution - is a thing somebody else may own. This invariant requires
# each one to carry a nameable licence in content/MEDIA-LICENCES.md, and it
# requires the manifest and the tree to agree in BOTH directions: a file with no
# row is a finding, and a row naming no file is a finding too.
#
# The precedent is worse than the requirement. Measured for the unit 003 plan
# (§2): neither published Drupal CMS site template documents any media licence
# at all - `haven` ships 22 Unsplash-named JPEGs and one GPL LICENSE.txt for the
# code. A template whose thesis is accountability cannot be the third.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS SCRIPT IS NOT RED ON THE DAY IT LANDS, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A HOLE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The contract in plan.md §6.1 says "scanned: N binaries; N must be > 0 or this
# script FAILS", citing I-028: a scan of zero files exits 0 exactly like a clean
# tree, and this repository has been caught by that twice.
#
# content/ holds ONE file today - a blank Canvas landing page - and ZERO media.
# Wave 10 (T-1007) authors the media. So a literal reading of that rule ships an
# invariant that is RED from the day it lands until a task three weeks away, and
# I-020 says a red that everybody has learned to step over is worse than no red
# at all. The other direction - printing "clean" over an empty scan - is the
# exact hazard the rule was written to prevent. Both options are wrong.
#
# The way out is to notice that "N binaries" is the SUBJECT of this scan, not
# its DENOMINATOR. The question I-028 actually asks is "did the scan happen?",
# and that question has an answer today:
#
#   * content/ is ENUMERATED in both scopes, and both enumerations must be > 0
#     or this script exits FATAL. Today that is 1 working / 1 packaged - the
#     Canvas page. This assertion is load-bearing NOW and can never be vacuous,
#     because a site template with an empty content/ is broken for other reasons.
#   * The CLASSIFIER is proved too: the enumerated entries are sorted into media
#     and non-media, and both halves are printed. A classifier that returned
#     nothing at all would take the enumeration to zero and hit the FATAL above.
#
# So the always-true denominator is the enumeration, and the media count is the
# subject it discriminates. On top of that the media rules ARM themselves:
#
#   ARMED  <=>  a media file exists in either scope, OR the manifest holds >= 1
#               data row.
#
# Unarmed, the summary line says NOT YET LOAD-BEARING in those words and exits
# 0. It never says "clean" over an empty scan. Armed, every rule below applies
# and `media files > 0` becomes an assertion exactly as §6.1 wrote it.
#
# Wave 10's first image arms it with no edit here and nobody having to remember.
# That last property is the whole point, and it is the same argument the theme
# repository's shared-invariants.manifest makes for installing its vacuous
# no-unstable-deps early: "whoever adds that dependency is the last person who
# will think to bring a guard along with it." The difference - and it is why
# this file argues the case at length rather than citing that one - is that the
# theme's vacuous invariant prints `require entries: 0` and stops there, while
# this one asserts a denominator that is positive today.
#
# Contract (shared by every invariant in tests/bin/):
#   scope:    the exact set of files inspected
#   scanned:  the content/ enumeration, and it must be > 0 or this script fails
#   findings: M, each printed as  path:line  reason
#   exit 0 if and only if M == 0
#
# TWO scopes, and they answer different questions - the pattern is
# no-code-in-template's, deliberately:
#   1. WORKING TREE (`find`). `git grep` and friends never see an uncommitted
#      offender (I-019), and an unlicensed image is at its most dangerous in the
#      minutes after it is dropped into the tree.
#   2. PACKAGED TREE (`git archive --format=tar HEAD | tar -tf -`). What a
#      marketplace reviewer downloads is the tarball, not the checkout. This is
#      the authoritative scope for the licence question, because it is the set
#      of files this project actually redistributes.
# A file present in one scope and absent from the other is itself a finding: it
# means either an uncommitted addition or an export-ignore that is quietly
# dropping media somebody wrote a licence row for.
#
# FATAL, not an ordinary finding, and printing NO summary line (I-029): content/
# absent or unreadable; either enumeration returning zero entries; `git archive`
# producing nothing; the allow-list being empty; the manifest file being absent
# while the scan is ARMED; any `grep` exiting >= 2. The closing metrics line is
# a claim about a scan that happened.
#
# House rules for tests/bin/ (T-321(c)): `wc -l` never `grep -c` for a compared
# value; numeric guards defaulted ONLY where zero means FAIL; grep rc >= 2 is an
# error, never "no match"; never `-F` with `-i`. The CANONICAL copy of the block,
# with the reasoning and the I-031 asymmetry spelled out, lives in the header of
# tests/bin/no-ci-allow-dev.
#
# FORBIDDEN: adding a path to an ignore list. A false positive is fixed by
# fixing the MANIFEST or by removing the file - never by shrinking the scope.
# This clause exists because the only route from a red deny-list back to green
# is deleting a term, and that is the move no-boilerplate's own FORBIDDEN clause
# was written to stop (T-703). The same applies to the allow-list of licences:
# a licence this project may not ship is removed from the FILE, never added to
# the list.
#
# Usage: tests/bin/media-licence   (run from anywhere; it cd's to the repo root)

set -u
# No `set -e`: a finding must be recorded and counted, not abort the run.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- repo root --
SCRIPT_DIR=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
REPO_ROOT=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$SCRIPT_DIR/../.." && pwd)
cd "$REPO_ROOT" || { echo "FATAL: cannot cd to repo root $REPO_ROOT"; exit 1; }

C_OK=""; C_BAD=""; C_OFF=""; C_DIM=""
if [ -t 1 ]; then
  C_OK=$'\033[32m'; C_BAD=$'\033[31m'; C_OFF=$'\033[0m'; C_DIM=$'\033[2m'
fi

# ------------------------------------------------- single source of truth ----
CONTENT_DIR='content'
MANIFEST='content/MEDIA-LICENCES.md'

# Files under content/ that are NOT media and therefore need no licence row.
# Declared by exact path, never by extension: a `*.md` glob would silently adopt
# the next Markdown file somebody drops in there, and the whole point of the
# closed world is that adopting a file is a decision somebody makes on purpose.
NON_MEDIA_DECLARED='content/MEDIA-LICENCES.md
content/PEOPLE.md'

# ------------------------------------------------------ the packaged ROOT ----
# T-1916. THIS SCRIPT USED TO STOP AT content/, AND THE PACKAGE DOES NOT.
# `logo.png` sat at the repository root for nineteen days: 2,251 bytes, shipped
# in every tarball, referenced by no recipe, no config and no content, covered
# by this invariant not at all, and carrying no provenance row anywhere. The
# sentence that stood in the manifest instead - "Nothing else in this package
# is a photograph" - was true and was prose: nothing could check it, and it did
# not mention the file.
#
# A binary with no licence row in a package heading for a marketplace review is
# the kind of thing a reviewer asks about, and "it was there already" is not an
# answer. So the closed world now covers the top level of the package too, and
# it is a CLOSED world rather than a list of two names: every file at the root,
# in either scope, must be on exactly one of the two lists below, or it is a
# finding. A new root binary therefore fires on the day it lands.
#
# BOTH LISTS ARE BY EXACT PATH, never by extension - the same rule the
# non-media list above follows, and for the same reason. `*.md` would silently
# adopt the next Markdown file somebody drops in, and adopting a file is a
# decision somebody makes on purpose.
#
# Root files that ARE media and need a manifest row.
ROOT_MEDIA_DECLARED='logo.png
screenshot.webp'

# Root files that are NOT media: the recipe, its metadata, the licence of the
# code and the prose a reader opens. None of them is a thing somebody else may
# own, which is the question this invariant asks.
# `.gitignore` and `.mailmap` are listed although T-1916 `export-ignore`s them:
# they are still in the WORKING tree, which is scope 1, and a classification
# that only held in one scope would be a hole shaped exactly like the one this
# block closes.
#
# ⚠️ FOUR OF THESE WERE NOT IN THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS LIST AND RULE 4 NAMED
# THEM ON ITS FIRST RUN: `.cspell-project-words.txt`, `.eslintrc.json`,
# `.gitlab-ci.yml` and `CLAUDE.md`. They are `export-ignore`d, so they are in
# scope 1 and not in scope 2 - which is exactly the case a list written by
# reading the tarball would have missed. The list was corrected from the
# invariant's own output rather than from memory, and that is the whole reason
# rule 4 enumerates a union of both scopes instead of the packaged one.
ROOT_NON_MEDIA_DECLARED='.cspell-project-words.txt
LICENCE-MANIFEST.md
.eslintrc.json
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.gitlab-ci.yml
.mailmap
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
LICENSE.txt
README.md
SECURITY.md
composer.json
recipe.yml
recommended.yml'

# The allow-list. PRINTED on every run, per §6.1: "A licence not on it is a
# finding, never a silent pass - an unrecognised string must not read as
# absence." Every entry is a licence this project may lawfully redistribute
# inside a GPL-2.0-or-later package.
ALLOWED_LICENCES='CC0-1.0
CC-BY-4.0
CC-BY-SA-4.0
OFL-1.1
own work'

FINDINGS=0
WORKDIR=$(mktemp -d -t media-licence.XXXXXX) || { echo "FATAL: mktemp -d failed"; exit 1; }
trap 'rm -rf "$WORKDIR"' EXIT INT TERM

finding() { # <file> <line> <reason>
  FINDINGS=$((FINDINGS + 1))
  printf '  %s%s:%s%s  %s\n' "$C_BAD" "${1#./}" "$2" "$C_OFF" "$3"
}

note() { printf '  %s%s%s\n' "$C_DIM" "$1" "$C_OFF"; }

fatal() { # <reason>
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s %s\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$1"
  printf 'This run has NO result, which is not a clean result (I-029).\n'
  exit 1
}

grep_fatal() { # <rc> <pattern> <subject>
  fatal "grep exited $1 on $3 while matching: $2 - a file that could not be searched is NOT a file that is correct (I-027)."
}

is_declared_non_media() { # <path>
  while IFS= read -r d; do
    [ -n "$d" ] || continue
    [ "$1" = "$d" ] && return 0
  done <<EOF
$NON_MEDIA_DECLARED
EOF
  return 1
}

is_declared_root_media() { # <path>
  while IFS= read -r d; do
    [ -n "$d" ] || continue
    [ "$1" = "$d" ] && return 0
  done <<EOF
$ROOT_MEDIA_DECLARED
EOF
  return 1
}

is_declared_root_non_media() { # <path>
  while IFS= read -r d; do
    [ -n "$d" ] || continue
    [ "$1" = "$d" ] && return 0
  done <<EOF
$ROOT_NON_MEDIA_DECLARED
EOF
  return 1
}

# A path is MEDIA when it is under content/, is not *.yml, is not *.json and is
# not a declared non-media file. Deliberately NOT "is it binary": T-1006 ships
# CSV distributions, which are text and are still somebody's data under somebody
# else's licence.
#
# T-1916 adds the second arm: a file at the TOP LEVEL of the package is media
# when it is on ROOT_MEDIA_DECLARED. The asymmetry with the content/ arm is
# deliberate. Under content/ the default is "media" and the exceptions are
# named, because content/ is where somebody else's work arrives; at the root
# the default is neither, because the root is where the recipe itself lives -
# so every root file is classified explicitly and rule 4 below fails on any
# that is not.
is_media() { # <path>
  case "$1" in
    */*) ;;
    *) is_declared_root_media "$1" && return 0
       return 1 ;;
  esac
  case "$1" in
    "$CONTENT_DIR"/*) ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  case "$1" in
    *.yml | *.json) return 1 ;;
  esac
  is_declared_non_media "$1" && return 1
  return 0
}

printf '=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'media-licence - Ágora - every shipped media file carries a nameable licence (T-904)\n'
printf 'repo: %s\n' "$REPO_ROOT"
printf 'date: %s\n' "$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')"
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

# ------------------------------------------------------------- preflight -----
for tool in find grep sort wc git tar; do
  command -v "$tool" >/dev/null 2>&1 || \
    fatal "required tool not available: $tool - the scan cannot be evaluated, and unevaluated is not clean."
done

[ -d "$CONTENT_DIR" ] || fatal "$CONTENT_DIR/ does not exist - a site template with no content/ is broken for reasons this script is not the right one to report."

ALLOWED_COUNT=0
while IFS= read -r l; do
  [ -n "$l" ] || continue
  ALLOWED_COUNT=$((ALLOWED_COUNT + 1))
done <<EOF
$ALLOWED_LICENCES
EOF
# Zero means FAIL here, which is the only direction in which the `:-0` default
# is safe (I-031). An empty allow-list would make every licence a finding, not
# a pass - but it would also mean the declaration had been emptied, and that is
# a state nobody should be able to reach quietly.
if [ "${ALLOWED_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'the licence allow-list is empty - the declaration has been emptied and this scan discriminates nothing (I-028).'
fi

ROOT_MEDIA_COUNT=0
while IFS= read -r l; do
  [ -n "$l" ] || continue
  ROOT_MEDIA_COUNT=$((ROOT_MEDIA_COUNT + 1))
done <<EOF
$ROOT_MEDIA_DECLARED
EOF
ROOT_NON_MEDIA_COUNT=0
while IFS= read -r l; do
  [ -n "$l" ] || continue
  ROOT_NON_MEDIA_COUNT=$((ROOT_NON_MEDIA_COUNT + 1))
done <<EOF
$ROOT_NON_MEDIA_DECLARED
EOF
# Both lists FATAL at zero, for the reason the allow-list does: an emptied
# classification list makes rule 4 report every root file as undeclared, or
# none of them, depending which half was emptied - and one of those two is a
# silent pass (I-028).
if [ "${ROOT_MEDIA_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ] || [ "${ROOT_NON_MEDIA_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'a root classification list is empty - rule 4 would classify the package root against nothing (I-028).'
fi

printf '\ndeclared scope\n'
note "content directory:   $CONTENT_DIR/"
note "manifest:            $MANIFEST"
note "non-media declared:  $(printf '%s' "$NON_MEDIA_DECLARED" | tr '\n' ' ')"
note "root media ($ROOT_MEDIA_COUNT):      $(printf '%s' "$ROOT_MEDIA_DECLARED" | tr '\n' ' ')"
note "root non-media ($ROOT_NON_MEDIA_COUNT):  $(printf '%s' "$ROOT_NON_MEDIA_DECLARED" | tr '\n' ' ')"
note "allow-list ($ALLOWED_COUNT):     $(printf '%s' "$ALLOWED_LICENCES" | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/  */ /g')"
note 'media = under content/, not *.yml, not *.json, not a declared non-media file'
note 'media = OR a top-level file on the root media list; every other top-level file must be on the root non-media list (rule 4)'

# =========================================== scope 1: the working tree ========
printf '\nscope 1 (early warning): working tree - find %s\n' "$CONTENT_DIR"
printf '          catches the uncommitted offender that scope 2 is structurally unable to see (I-019)\n'

find "$CONTENT_DIR" -type f -print 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^\./||' | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORKDIR/work-all"
WORK_ENTRIES=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/work-all" | tr -d ' ')
printf 'content entries (working): %s\n' "$WORK_ENTRIES"
# The always-positive denominator. Zero means FAIL, so `:-0` is the safe
# direction (I-031). This is the assertion that makes an unarmed run honest.
if [ "${WORK_ENTRIES:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal "$CONTENT_DIR/ enumerated 0 files in the working tree - the scan did not happen (I-007/I-028)."
fi

# T-1916: the top level of the working tree, enumerated separately so rule 4
# has a set to classify and so the root media join the media union below.
find . -maxdepth 1 -type f -print 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^\./||' | LC_ALL=C sort \
  > "$WORKDIR/work-root"
WORK_ROOT=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/work-root" | tr -d ' ')
printf 'root entries (working):    %s\n' "$WORK_ROOT"
# Zero means FAIL (I-031): this repository always has recipe.yml at its root,
# so a zero here is a broken enumeration and not an empty root.
if [ "${WORK_ROOT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'the top level of the working tree enumerated 0 files - recipe.yml is always there, so the enumeration broke (I-007/I-028).'
fi
cat "$WORKDIR/work-all" "$WORKDIR/work-root" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$WORKDIR/work-scope"

: > "$WORKDIR/work-media"
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  is_media "$f" && printf '%s\n' "$f" >> "$WORKDIR/work-media"
done < "$WORKDIR/work-scope"
WORK_MEDIA=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/work-media" | tr -d ' ')
printf 'binaries (working):        %s\n' "$WORK_MEDIA"

# =========================================== scope 2: the packaged tree =======
printf '\nscope 2 (authoritative): packaged tree - git archive --format=tar HEAD | tar -tf -\n'
printf '          what a marketplace reviewer downloads is the tarball, not the checkout\n'

git rev-parse --verify HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1 || fatal 'no git HEAD - the packaged scope cannot be resolved, and an unresolvable scope is not an empty one.'
ARCHIVE_LIST=$(git archive --format=tar HEAD 2>/dev/null | tar -tf - 2>/dev/null)
ARCHIVE_RC=$?
if [ "$ARCHIVE_RC" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$ARCHIVE_LIST" ]; then
  fatal "git archive HEAD produced nothing (exit $ARCHIVE_RC) - an empty archive would pass every check here through the back door."
fi

PKG_MATCHED=$(printf '%s\n' "$ARCHIVE_LIST" | grep -E "^$CONTENT_DIR/" 2>/dev/null)
PKG_RC=$?
[ "$PKG_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$PKG_RC" "^$CONTENT_DIR/" 'the archive listing'
printf '%s\n' "$PKG_MATCHED" | grep -v '/$' 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^$' 2>/dev/null \
  | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORKDIR/pkg-all"
PKG_ENTRIES=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/pkg-all" | tr -d ' ')
printf 'content entries (packaged): %s\n' "$PKG_ENTRIES"
# Same assertion, other scope. Zero means FAIL (I-031).
if [ "${PKG_ENTRIES:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal "$CONTENT_DIR/ contributes 0 files to the packaged tree - either content/ became export-ignored or the archive broke; either way this run proves nothing (I-028)."
fi

# T-1916: the top level of the tarball. `grep -v /` keeps only entries with no
# slash at all, which is both the files at the root and nothing else - a
# directory entry always ends in one.
printf '%s\n' "$ARCHIVE_LIST" | grep -v '/' 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^$' 2>/dev/null \
  | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORKDIR/pkg-root"
PKG_ROOT=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/pkg-root" | tr -d ' ')
printf 'root entries (packaged):    %s\n' "$PKG_ROOT"
# Zero means FAIL (I-031): a tarball with nothing at its top level has no
# recipe.yml and is not a site template.
if [ "${PKG_ROOT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'the tarball has 0 files at its top level - recipe.yml is mandatory there, so the archive listing was not read (I-028).'
fi
cat "$WORKDIR/pkg-all" "$WORKDIR/pkg-root" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$WORKDIR/pkg-scope"

: > "$WORKDIR/pkg-media"
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  is_media "$f" && printf '%s\n' "$f" >> "$WORKDIR/pkg-media"
done < "$WORKDIR/pkg-scope"
PKG_MEDIA=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/pkg-media" | tr -d ' ')
printf 'binaries (packaged):        %s\n' "$PKG_MEDIA"

# The union is what gets a licence row: a file in either scope is a file this
# project is responsible for.
sort -u "$WORKDIR/work-media" "$WORKDIR/pkg-media" > "$WORKDIR/media-union"
MEDIA_UNION=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/media-union" | tr -d ' ')

# Third set, used only by rule 3: what is COMMITTED at HEAD, which is not the
# same question as what the archive contains - `export-ignore` sits between the
# two, and the gap between them is a defect nothing else in tests/bin/ can see.
: > "$WORKDIR/tracked-media"
# T-1916: the whole tree rather than content/ alone, so a root media file that
# is committed but dropped from the tarball is caught by rule 3 the same way a
# content/ one is. `is_media` filters it back down, so the set is unchanged for
# every path this invariant did not previously consider.
TRACKED_LIST=$(git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD 2>/dev/null)
TRACKED_RC=$?
[ "$TRACKED_RC" -ne 0 ] && fatal "git ls-tree -r HEAD failed (exit $TRACKED_RC) - rule 3 would compare against an empty set and report agreement it never measured."
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  is_media "$f" && printf '%s\n' "$f" >> "$WORKDIR/tracked-media"
done <<EOF
$TRACKED_LIST
EOF
TRACKED_MEDIA=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/tracked-media" | tr -d ' ')
printf 'binaries (committed at HEAD): %s\n' "$TRACKED_MEDIA"

# ================================================ the manifest ================
printf '\nmanifest - %s\n' "$MANIFEST"

MANIFEST_ROWS=0
: > "$WORKDIR/rows"
# A data row is a table row whose first cell is a path under content/ or one of
# the DECLARED root media paths. The header row and the `|---|` separator
# therefore exclude themselves, and no line-number arithmetic is needed to skip
# them - nor does the `| column | what goes in it |` table further up the
# manifest become a data row by accident.
#
# T-1916: the alternation is built from the DECLARATION and not from the disk.
# Deriving it from the files that exist would make a row whose file has been
# deleted stop being a row at all, which is the precise case rule 2 exists to
# catch - a stale row would go quiet instead of red.
ROW_PREFIX_RE="$CONTENT_DIR/"
while IFS= read -r r; do
  [ -n "$r" ] || continue
  ROW_PREFIX_RE="$ROW_PREFIX_RE|$(printf '%s' "$r" | sed 's/[].[^$*+?(){}|\\]/\\&/g')"
done <<EOF
$ROOT_MEDIA_DECLARED
EOF
if [ -r "$MANIFEST" ]; then
  ROW_MATCHED=$(grep -nE "^\|[[:space:]]*($ROW_PREFIX_RE)" "$MANIFEST" 2>/dev/null)
  ROW_RC=$?
  [ "$ROW_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$ROW_RC" "^\\|[[:space:]]*($ROW_PREFIX_RE)" "$MANIFEST"
  if [ "$ROW_RC" -eq 0 ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$ROW_MATCHED" > "$WORKDIR/rows"
    MANIFEST_ROWS=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/rows" | tr -d ' ')
  fi
  note "present, $MANIFEST_ROWS data row(s)"
else
  note 'absent'
fi

# The first cell of every data row, one per line. Built as a PRE-PASS so rule 1
# can ask "is this file listed?" with `grep -qxF` against a literal set, rather
# than building a regex out of a filename - a path holding a `.` or a `+` would
# otherwise match files it does not name, and the failure would be a silent PASS.
: > "$WORKDIR/row-paths"
while IFS= read -r rec; do
  [ -n "$rec" ] || continue
  _row=${rec#*:}
  _body=${_row#|}
  _first=${_body%%|*}
  _first="${_first#"${_first%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
  _first="${_first%"${_first##*[![:space:]]}"}"
  printf '%s\n' "$_first" >> "$WORKDIR/row-paths"
done < "$WORKDIR/rows"

# ================================================ arming ======================
# ARMED <=> a media file exists in either scope, OR the manifest holds a row.
# Both halves matter: the first arms on wave 10's first image, the second arms
# on a manifest row whose file was deleted - which is exactly dirty case (b)
# taken to its limit and must not read as "nothing to do here".
ARMED=no
if [ "${MEDIA_UNION:-0}" -gt 0 ] || [ "${MANIFEST_ROWS:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
  ARMED=yes
fi

printf '\nstate: %s\n' "$([ "$ARMED" = yes ] && echo 'ARMED - every media rule below applies' || echo 'NOT YET LOAD-BEARING - no media and no manifest rows')"

if [ "$ARMED" = yes ] && [ ! -r "$MANIFEST" ]; then
  fatal "$MANIFEST is absent or unreadable while media files exist - every row check below would be vacuous, and a vacuous pass over shipped media is the failure this invariant exists to prevent (I-029)."
fi

# ================================================ the rules ===================
if [ "$ARMED" = yes ]; then
  printf '\n1. every media file has a manifest row\n'
  R1=0
  while IFS= read -r f; do
    [ -n "$f" ] || continue
    if ! grep -qxF "$f" "$WORKDIR/row-paths" 2>/dev/null; then
      # T-1916: the reason names the right place. A root file is not "shipped
      # under content/", and a finding that misdescribes where the file is
      # sends the next reader to the wrong directory.
      case "$f" in
        */*) _where="shipped under $CONTENT_DIR/" ;;
        *)   _where='shipped at the root of the package' ;;
      esac
      finding "$f" '0' "$_where with no row in $MANIFEST - somebody may own this file and the package does not say who"
      R1=$((R1 + 1))
    fi
  done < "$WORKDIR/media-union"
  [ "$R1" -eq 0 ] && note "all $MEDIA_UNION media file(s) are listed"

  printf '\n2. every manifest row names an existing media file, and its six cells are filled\n'
  R2=0
  while IFS= read -r rec; do
    [ -n "$rec" ] || continue
    _ln=${rec%%:*}
    _row=${rec#*:}
    # Strip the leading and trailing pipe, then split on the remaining ones.
    _body=${_row#|}
    _body=${_body%|}
    _path=''; _title=''; _author=''; _source=''; _licence=''; _date=''
    _i=0
    # A trailing pipe is appended so the last cell terminates like every other
    # one. Without it the loop needs a "no pipe left" branch, and that branch is
    # where an empty final cell - exactly the "empty date retrieved" case rule 2
    # is meant to catch - silently disappears.
    _rest="$_body|"
    while [ -n "$_rest" ]; do
      _cell=${_rest%%|*}
      _rest=${_rest#*|}
      # Trim surrounding whitespace without an external process.
      _cell="${_cell#"${_cell%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
      _cell="${_cell%"${_cell##*[![:space:]]}"}"
      _i=$((_i + 1))
      case "$_i" in
        1) _path=$_cell ;;
        2) _title=$_cell ;;
        3) _author=$_cell ;;
        4) _source=$_cell ;;
        5) _licence=$_cell ;;
        6) _date=$_cell ;;
      esac
    done

    if [ "$_i" -ne 6 ]; then
      finding "$MANIFEST" "$_ln" "row has $_i cell(s), expected 6 (path | title | author | source URL | SPDX id or \"own work\" | date retrieved)"
      R2=$((R2 + 1))
      continue
    fi

    if ! grep -qxF "$_path" "$WORKDIR/media-union" 2>/dev/null; then
      finding "$MANIFEST" "$_ln" "row names [$_path], which is not a media file in either scope - the row has outlived its file, or the path is mistyped"
      R2=$((R2 + 1))
    fi
    for pair in "title:$_title" "author:$_author" "source URL:$_source" "date retrieved:$_date"; do
      _label=${pair%%:*}
      _value=${pair#*:}
      if [ -z "$_value" ]; then
        finding "$MANIFEST" "$_ln" "empty $_label for [$_path] - an empty attribution field is a claim nobody can check"
        R2=$((R2 + 1))
      fi
    done

    _ok=no
    while IFS= read -r a; do
      [ -n "$a" ] || continue
      [ "$_licence" = "$a" ] && _ok=yes
    done <<EOF
$ALLOWED_LICENCES
EOF
    if [ "$_ok" != yes ]; then
      finding "$MANIFEST" "$_ln" "licence [$_licence] is not on the allow-list ($(printf '%s' "$ALLOWED_LICENCES" | tr '\n' '/' | sed 's|/$||')) - an unrecognised string must not read as absence. Remove the FILE or relicense it; the list is not the thing that moves"
      R2=$((R2 + 1))
    fi
  done < "$WORKDIR/rows"
  [ "$R2" -eq 0 ] && note "all $MANIFEST_ROWS row(s) resolve, are complete and name an allow-listed licence"

  printf '\n3. the two scopes agree\n'
  # THREE sets, not two, and the third is what keeps this rule from crying wolf
  # through the whole of wave 10. TRACKED is `git ls-tree HEAD`, which sees a
  # committed file whether or not `export-ignore` keeps it out of the tarball;
  # PACKAGED is the archive. So:
  #   tracked but not packaged  -> an export-ignore is dropping shipped media.
  #                                A real defect, and one nothing else catches.
  #   packaged but not working  -> the tarball ships a file nobody here can open.
  #   working but not tracked   -> the file is simply not committed yet. That is
  #                                the normal state of a file being authored, and
  #                                calling it a finding would make the invariant
  #                                red for the entire duration of every authoring
  #                                session - which is how a red gets ignored
  #                                (I-020). Counted and PRINTED, never silent.
  R3=0
  UNCOMMITTED=0
  while IFS= read -r f; do
    [ -n "$f" ] || continue
    if grep -qxF "$f" "$WORKDIR/tracked-media" 2>/dev/null; then
      grep -qxF "$f" "$WORKDIR/pkg-media" 2>/dev/null || {
        finding "$f" '0' 'committed media that the packaged tarball does not contain - an export-ignore rule is dropping a file this manifest says we redistribute'
        R3=$((R3 + 1))
      }
    else
      UNCOMMITTED=$((UNCOMMITTED + 1))
      note "$f: present in the working tree, not committed yet (not a finding; counted)"
    fi
  done < "$WORKDIR/work-media"
  while IFS= read -r f; do
    [ -n "$f" ] || continue
    grep -qxF "$f" "$WORKDIR/work-media" 2>/dev/null || {
      finding "$f" '0' 'media in the packaged tree but absent from the working tree - the tarball ships a file nobody here can inspect'
      R3=$((R3 + 1))
    }
  done < "$WORKDIR/pkg-media"
  [ "$R3" -eq 0 ] && note "committed media sets agree; $UNCOMMITTED uncommitted file(s)"

  # ------------------------------------------------------------- rule 4 -----
  # T-1916. The closed world over the top level of the package. Every root file
  # in either scope is on exactly one of the two declared lists, and every path
  # on the root media list exists in at least one scope.
  #
  # The second half is not decoration: without it a declaration could name a
  # file that left the package, the manifest row for it would still be detected
  # (the alternation is built from the declaration, above), rule 2 would report
  # it as a row naming no file - and a reader would have no way to tell whether
  # the file or the declaration was the mistake. This says which.
  printf '\n4. every top-level file is classified, and every declared root media file exists\n'
  R4=0
  ROOT_SCOPE=0
  ROOT_AS_MEDIA=0
  ROOT_AS_NON_MEDIA=0
  cat "$WORKDIR/work-root" "$WORKDIR/pkg-root" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$WORKDIR/root-union"
  while IFS= read -r f; do
    [ -n "$f" ] || continue
    ROOT_SCOPE=$((ROOT_SCOPE + 1))
    if is_declared_root_media "$f"; then
      ROOT_AS_MEDIA=$((ROOT_AS_MEDIA + 1))
      if is_declared_root_non_media "$f"; then
        finding "$f" '0' 'a top-level file is on BOTH root lists - the classification says two things at once'
        R4=$((R4 + 1))
      fi
    elif is_declared_root_non_media "$f"; then
      ROOT_AS_NON_MEDIA=$((ROOT_AS_NON_MEDIA + 1))
    else
      finding "$f" '0' 'a top-level file of this package is on neither root list - classify it in tests/bin/media-licence as root media (and give it a manifest row) or as root non-media, or take it out of the package'
      R4=$((R4 + 1))
    fi
  done < "$WORKDIR/root-union"
  # Zero means FAIL (I-031): the union of two enumerations that each FATAL at
  # zero cannot legitimately be empty.
  if [ "${ROOT_SCOPE:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
    fatal 'rule 4 classified 0 top-level files - the root enumerations did not reach it (I-028).'
  fi
  while IFS= read -r d; do
    [ -n "$d" ] || continue
    if ! grep -qxF "$d" "$WORKDIR/root-union" 2>/dev/null; then
      finding "$MANIFEST" '0' "[$d] is declared as root media in tests/bin/media-licence and exists in neither scope - the declaration has outlived its file"
      R4=$((R4 + 1))
    fi
  done <<EOF
$ROOT_MEDIA_DECLARED
EOF
  [ "$R4" -eq 0 ] && note "all $ROOT_SCOPE top-level file(s) classified: $ROOT_AS_MEDIA media, $ROOT_AS_NON_MEDIA non-media"
else
  printf '\n1-4. skipped: there is no media to license and no row claiming there is\n'
  note 'this is NOT a clean scan of media - it is the absence of media, and the summary line says so in those words'
  note "the rules arm themselves on wave 10's first image (T-1007); no edit here, nothing to remember"
fi

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- summary ---
printf '\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n'
printf 'content entries: %s (working) · %s (packaged)\n' "$WORK_ENTRIES" "$PKG_ENTRIES"
printf 'root files: %s (working) · %s (packaged) · root media declared: %s · root non-media declared: %s\n' \
  "$WORK_ROOT" "$PKG_ROOT" "$ROOT_MEDIA_COUNT" "$ROOT_NON_MEDIA_COUNT"
printf '%s binaries (working) · %s binaries (packaged) · %s manifest rows · %s allow-listed licences · %s findings' \
  "$WORK_MEDIA" "$PKG_MEDIA" "$MANIFEST_ROWS" "$ALLOWED_COUNT" "$FINDINGS"
if [ "$ARMED" = yes ]; then
  printf '\n'
else
  printf ' · NOT YET LOAD-BEARING\n'
fi

if [ "$FINDINGS" -eq 0 ]; then
  # The word "clean" is reserved for a scan that had something to scan. An
  # unarmed run prints a different sentence on purpose: "clean" over an empty
  # set is the exact string I-028 says a degenerate run must never be able to
  # counterfeit, and a reader skimming CI output reads the last line.
  if [ "$ARMED" = yes ]; then
    printf '%s%s%s\n' "$C_OK" 'media-licence: clean' "$C_OFF"
  else
    printf '%s%s%s\n' "$C_DIM" 'media-licence: 0 findings - NOT YET LOAD-BEARING, there is no media in either scope' "$C_OFF"
  fi
  exit 0
fi
printf '%s%s%s%s%s\n' "$C_BAD" 'media-licence: ' "$FINDINGS" ' finding(s)' "$C_OFF"
exit 1
